Subject: Binary Time-Varying Predictor in Panel Model
Message-ID: <xPFkHjXPrKc.ebzXkIXVCQAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/xPFkHjXPrKc/m/ebzXkIXVCQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Lavaan handles time-invariant categorical predictors well. Imagine performance measured on 600 people across 10 time points modeled with a simple autoregressive panel model (Bollen & Brand, 2010): performance (continuous) at time t is regressed on performance at time t-1 and gender ('male' or 'female'):

performance.2 ~ ap*performance.1 + b*gender

performance.3 ~ ap*performance.2 + b*gender

...etc. (unobserved heterogeneity and residuals not shown)

Again, lavaan functions smoothly. But now imagine that participants were either sent an email (email = 1) or not (email = 0) at each time point, and this was thought to influence performance.  The wide data set therefore contains performance at each time point (e.g., performance.1, performance.2, performance.3, etc.) and email at each time point, coded as a binary 0 or 1 (email.1, email.2, email.3, etc.). The model then takes the form:

performance.2 ~ ap*performance.1 + b*email.2

performance.3 ~ ap*performance.2 + b*email.3

...etc.

Running this second model results in the error: "some variables have no values (only missings) or no variance." Are there better ways to incorporate a binary, time-varying predictor?

Any help would be greatly appreciated.

Christopher

